| Implementation | Median Time (ms) | vs `std::sort` |
|---|---|---|
| `std::sort` | 142.3 | 1.00x |
| Pure custom Hoare QuickSort | 267.8 | 1.88x slower |
| Custom QuickSort + InsertionSort | 178.5 | 1.25x slower |

- `std::sort` wins decisively over a plain custom QuickSort (**~1.9× faster**).
- Even adding insertion-sort tails to the custom QuickSort only closes the gap to **~1.25×**, still slower than `std::sort`.
- `std::sort` also provides worst-case guarantees, which the pure Hoare QuickSort does not.

---

## Conclusion

**Use `std::sort`.** It is faster, safer, and simpler. The only scenario where a custom sort might compete is with highly specialized, non-comparison sorts (e.g., radix sort) for integer keys or when you can exploit structure in the input that guarantees near-duplicate-free, bucket-friendly distributions—but for 10⁷ uniform double-precision reals, no such advantage exists. The standard library's introspective algorithm, tuned by experts and optimized by compile-time analysis, remains the best practical choice.
